1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:01,890
Léa: Hello everyone!

2
00:00:01,890 --> 00:00:04,200
Welcome to The Cozy French,

3
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,090
our cozy little corner

4
00:00:06,090 --> 00:00:07,800
to learn French

5
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:10,800
through everyday conversations.

6
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:14,350
I'm Léa, and I'm delighted to see you again.

7
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:15,700
Mathieu: And I'm Mathieu!

8
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:17,350
Hello, dear friends!

9
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:18,900
Whether you're on the bus,

10
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:20,800
at home or out for a walk,

11
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:22,050
make yourselves at home.

12
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:24,400
Today, I hope you've kept

13
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:25,700
a little room

14
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:28,210
for a conversation that feels good.

15
00:00:28,260 --> 00:00:31,600
Léa: Mathieu, you look especially happy.

16
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:32,950
Good news?

17
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:34,250
Mathieu: Not exactly.

18
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:38,200
This morning, I spilled coffee on my shirt,

19
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:42,500
missed my tram and forgot my umbrella.

20
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:45,050
An absolutely wonderful morning!

21
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:48,680
Léa: Wait... You just described three problems.

22
00:00:48,680 --> 00:00:49,950
Why are you smiling?

23
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,200
Mathieu: Because after missing the tram,

24
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:54,410
I went into a bakery.

25
00:00:54,460 --> 00:00:57,410
The baker gave me a little cookie

26
00:00:57,410 --> 00:00:58,500
with my coffee.

27
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:00,170
And then I ran into a neighbor

28
00:01:00,170 --> 00:01:02,700
I hadn't seen in a long time.

29
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:05,900
Léa: So your morning didn't start as planned,

30
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:07,190
but it had

31
00:01:07,190 --> 00:01:09,300
a few nice surprises in store for you.

32
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:11,500
Mathieu: That's it. I won't pretend

33
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:14,200
I was thrilled about my stained shirt.

34
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:16,500
But I decided not to let

35
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:19,400
that accident ruin the whole day.

36
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:22,230
Léa: You know, that's a great introduction

37
00:01:22,230 --> 00:01:23,110
to our topic:

38
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:25,580
"The Power of Positivity",

39
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:28,200
the power of positive thinking.

40
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:30,600
Mathieu: Ah! So today,

41
00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:32,100
we're going to learn to smile

42
00:01:32,100 --> 00:01:33,750
when the tram leaves without us?

43
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:34,850
Léa: Not at all!

44
00:01:34,900 --> 00:01:37,900
We'll talk about a more realistic attitude:

45
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:39,910
recognizing difficulties,

46
00:01:39,910 --> 00:01:42,700
without forgetting what can still help us,

47
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:45,800
surprise us or give us hope.

48
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:47,910
Mathieu: I like that definition.

49
00:01:47,910 --> 00:01:50,080
Because if being positive

50
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:52,310
means being cheerful all the time,

51
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:54,800
I give up on Monday morning.

52
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:57,100
Léa: And nobody's asking you to.

53
00:01:57,100 --> 00:01:59,400
We'll discuss our thoughts,

54
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:01,200
our little happy moments,

55
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:03,780
our goals and the way

56
00:02:03,780 --> 00:02:06,400
to get through harder times.

57
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:08,300
Mathieu: With our usual stories,

58
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:12,200
a few jokes and accessible French.

59
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:14,500
Near the end, we'll also come back

60
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:16,900
to several useful expressions.

61
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:18,190
Léa: Before we start,

62
00:02:18,190 --> 00:02:20,310
tell us in the comments:

63
00:02:20,360 --> 00:02:21,590
what little thing

64
00:02:21,590 --> 00:02:23,550
made you smile recently?

65
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:26,300
One simple sentence in French is enough.

66
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:28,700
Mathieu: And if you like these conversations,

67
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:31,600
remember to subscribe to The Cozy French.

68
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:33,950
Come on, Léa, I'm ready.

69
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:35,450
Well, almost.

70
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:38,450
I still have a piece of cookie left.

71
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:40,730
Léa: Mathieu! Finish your cookie

72
00:02:40,730 --> 00:02:43,200
and tell me something.

73
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:45,120
When your coffee spilled,

74
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:47,310
what was your first thought?

75
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:49,540
Mathieu: "Of course! It's starting badly,

76
00:02:49,540 --> 00:02:52,400
so the whole day will be bad."

77
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:54,100
It was automatic.

78
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:55,820
Léa: And that's interesting,

79
00:02:55,820 --> 00:02:57,600
because one unpleasant event

80
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:00,310
can sometimes become, in our heads,

81
00:03:00,310 --> 00:03:02,600
a conclusion about everything else.

82
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:05,440
Mathieu: Exactly. I'd only lived

83
00:03:05,440 --> 00:03:07,400
fifteen minutes of my day,

84
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:10,900
but my mind had already decided the rest.

85
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:12,600
Léa: That happens to me too.

86
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:14,410
When one thing goes wrong,

87
00:03:14,460 --> 00:03:16,290
I notice more easily

88
00:03:16,290 --> 00:03:18,200
the other little problems.

89
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:19,870
As if my attention were looking for

90
00:03:19,870 --> 00:03:22,400
proof that I'm right to worry.

91
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:23,760
Mathieu: So how do you

92
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:25,650
avoid falling into that trap?

93
00:03:25,700 --> 00:03:29,200
Léa: I try to describe the situation precisely.

94
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:31,500
Instead of saying "Everything's going wrong",

95
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:35,000
I say "This morning, I had a setback."

96
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:36,450
Mathieu: "A setback"?

97
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:38,200
That's a word I really like.

98
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:41,100
Léa: A setback is an unexpected event

99
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:43,110
that disrupts our plans.

100
00:03:43,160 --> 00:03:46,070
For example: "Something came up

101
00:03:46,070 --> 00:03:47,900
and I'll be a bit late."

102
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:50,200
Mathieu: That's much less dramatic

103
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:52,490
than "My life is a disaster

104
00:03:52,490 --> 00:03:54,200
because I missed the tram."

105
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:55,450
Léa: Exactly.

106
00:03:55,500 --> 00:03:56,910
The words we choose

107
00:03:56,910 --> 00:03:58,800
don't get rid of the problem,

108
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:00,350
but they can help us

109
00:04:00,350 --> 00:04:02,200
look at it more fairly.

110
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:03,640
Mathieu: You know, I have a friend

111
00:04:03,640 --> 00:04:06,100
who has a funny expression.

112
00:04:06,100 --> 00:04:09,000
When a day starts badly, he says:

113
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:10,850
"Well, this isn't the script

114
00:04:10,850 --> 00:04:12,110
I ordered."

115
00:04:12,160 --> 00:04:14,170
Léa: I love it! He admits

116
00:04:14,170 --> 00:04:15,900
that something's wrong,

117
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:17,700
without turning it into a tragedy.

118
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:19,420
Mathieu: Yes. And sometimes,

119
00:04:19,420 --> 00:04:21,550
that's enough to breathe

120
00:04:21,550 --> 00:04:24,000
and look for a solution.

121
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,500
A stained shirt can be changed.

122
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:29,500
A missed tram? There'll be another one.

123
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:31,050
Léa: Of course, this reasoning

124
00:04:31,050 --> 00:04:33,200
doesn't work so simply

125
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:35,300
for big difficulties.

126
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:37,030
A loss, an illness

127
00:04:37,030 --> 00:04:40,110
or a difficult financial situation

128
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:41,390
call for something other

129
00:04:41,390 --> 00:04:43,400
than a funny little phrase.

130
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:45,500
Mathieu: It's important to say that.

131
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:47,300
You can be positive

132
00:04:47,300 --> 00:04:50,300
without minimizing what someone's going through.

133
00:04:50,300 --> 00:04:51,750
Léa: Exactly.

134
00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:54,500
And for small everyday annoyances,

135
00:04:54,500 --> 00:04:56,810
slightly changing perspective

136
00:04:56,810 --> 00:04:59,100
can already make a difference.

137
00:04:59,100 --> 00:05:00,800
Mathieu: Like looking at the window

138
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:03,800
instead of only looking at the coffee stain?

139
00:05:03,800 --> 00:05:06,610
Léa: As long as you clean the stain, though!

140
00:05:06,660 --> 00:05:07,890
Mathieu: You're right.

141
00:05:07,890 --> 00:05:09,460
Positive thinking

142
00:05:09,460 --> 00:05:12,400
doesn't make the coffee disappear.

143
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:15,280
Léa: No, but it can help you not give

144
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:17,700
that incident all your energy.

145
00:05:17,700 --> 00:05:20,580
And speaking of which, what do you do when you wake up

146
00:05:20,580 --> 00:05:22,950
with lots of negative thoughts?

147
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,800
Mathieu: Usually, I pick up my phone.

148
00:05:25,800 --> 00:05:28,400
Very bad idea, I know.

149
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:29,350
Léa: Why?

150
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:32,310
Mathieu: I check messages, then the news,

151
00:05:32,360 --> 00:05:34,190
then photos of people

152
00:05:34,190 --> 00:05:37,700
who seem to have absolutely perfect lives.

153
00:05:37,700 --> 00:05:39,170
Ten minutes later,

154
00:05:39,170 --> 00:05:41,300
I feel even more tired.

155
00:05:41,300 --> 00:05:43,700
Léa: It's like starting your day

156
00:05:43,700 --> 00:05:45,600
by letting others choose

157
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:47,700
what will fill your mind.

158
00:05:47,700 --> 00:05:49,660
Mathieu: Exactly.

159
00:05:49,710 --> 00:05:52,600
So I tried something new:

160
00:05:52,600 --> 00:05:54,780
for the first ten minutes of the morning,

161
00:05:54,780 --> 00:05:56,900
I leave my phone aside.

162
00:05:56,900 --> 00:05:59,510
Léa: And what do you do instead?

163
00:05:59,560 --> 00:06:00,940
Mathieu: I open the window,

164
00:06:00,940 --> 00:06:03,080
I make my breakfast,

165
00:06:03,080 --> 00:06:05,900
and I choose some nice music.

166
00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:08,210
Not a heroic program.

167
00:06:08,210 --> 00:06:11,400
Just a gentler start to the day.

168
00:06:11,400 --> 00:06:14,050
Léa: Does it change your mood?

169
00:06:14,100 --> 00:06:15,400
Mathieu: Not every time.

170
00:06:15,400 --> 00:06:18,000
Some mornings, I'm still worried.

171
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:18,870
But at least,

172
00:06:18,870 --> 00:06:21,800
I don't start by adding twenty pieces of news

173
00:06:21,800 --> 00:06:24,100
to an already very full head.

174
00:06:24,100 --> 00:06:26,510
Léa: That's an interesting approach.

175
00:06:26,560 --> 00:06:27,560
Being positive

176
00:06:27,560 --> 00:06:30,700
isn't controlling all your emotions.

177
00:06:30,700 --> 00:06:32,260
Sometimes it's choosing

178
00:06:32,260 --> 00:06:34,800
what you give your attention to.

179
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:37,700
Mathieu: And sometimes, the most positive choice

180
00:06:37,700 --> 00:06:40,900
might be eating a real breakfast

181
00:06:40,900 --> 00:06:43,120
instead of immediately answering

182
00:06:43,120 --> 00:06:44,600
three emails.

183
00:06:44,600 --> 00:06:45,840
Léa: Absolutely.

184
00:06:45,890 --> 00:06:48,400
But I'd like to add a nuance:

185
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:51,240
you don't need to turn every morning

186
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:53,810
into a perfect routine to feel okay.

187
00:06:53,860 --> 00:06:54,860
Mathieu: Thank goodness.

188
00:06:54,910 --> 00:06:56,500
As soon as a routine

189
00:06:56,500 --> 00:06:58,500
has more than four steps,

190
00:06:58,500 --> 00:07:00,500
I need an instruction manual!

191
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:02,800
Léa: Then let's keep things simple.

192
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:06,300
Sometimes, five calm minutes are enough.

193
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:09,700
And sometimes, we don't even have those five minutes.

194
00:07:09,700 --> 00:07:11,400
That's not a failure.

195
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:14,300
Mathieu: By the way, speaking of negative thoughts,

196
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:15,950
you told me recently

197
00:07:15,950 --> 00:07:19,110
you'd had a pretty stressful period.

198
00:07:19,160 --> 00:07:20,990
Léa: Yes. A few months ago,

199
00:07:20,990 --> 00:07:22,860
I was working on a project

200
00:07:22,860 --> 00:07:24,900
that I found important.

201
00:07:24,900 --> 00:07:27,300
I had a lot of responsibilities

202
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:30,200
and felt I was never doing enough.

203
00:07:30,200 --> 00:07:31,490
Mathieu: You'd go home

204
00:07:31,490 --> 00:07:33,950
and keep thinking about work?

205
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:35,450
Léa: Exactly.

206
00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:38,280
In the evening, I'd replay in my head

207
00:07:38,280 --> 00:07:40,600
the day's conversations.

208
00:07:40,600 --> 00:07:43,700
I wondered if I'd forgotten something,

209
00:07:43,700 --> 00:07:45,600
if a colleague was unhappy,

210
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:47,210
if I was going to make a mistake.

211
00:07:47,260 --> 00:07:50,500
Mathieu: Ah, the brain holding a meeting

212
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:53,300
when everyone has left the office.

213
00:07:53,300 --> 00:07:54,250
Léa: That's it.

214
00:07:54,300 --> 00:07:56,900
One evening, I took a sheet of paper

215
00:07:56,900 --> 00:07:59,000
and drew two columns.

216
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:00,540
Mathieu: A sheet of paper?

217
00:08:00,590 --> 00:08:02,550
Not a new app?

218
00:08:02,600 --> 00:08:03,760
Léa: No, Mathieu.

219
00:08:03,760 --> 00:08:07,300
Sometimes, paper works very well!

220
00:08:07,300 --> 00:08:10,250
Mathieu: Okay, I'm listening. What did you write?

221
00:08:10,300 --> 00:08:12,500
Léa: In the first column,

222
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:15,710
I wrote down what I could act on.

223
00:08:15,760 --> 00:08:19,300
In the second, what didn't depend on me.

224
00:08:19,300 --> 00:08:20,450
Mathieu: For example?

225
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:22,700
Léa: I could prepare my meeting,

226
00:08:22,700 --> 00:08:26,400
ask for details and meet my deadlines.

227
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:28,930
On the other hand, I couldn't guess

228
00:08:28,930 --> 00:08:30,450
what everyone was thinking

229
00:08:30,450 --> 00:08:32,900
or prevent every surprise.

230
00:08:32,900 --> 00:08:35,220
Mathieu: And seeing that difference on paper

231
00:08:35,220 --> 00:08:35,950
helped you?

232
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:39,400
Léa: Yes. It didn't erase my worries,

233
00:08:39,400 --> 00:08:40,490
but I understood

234
00:08:40,490 --> 00:08:42,760
that I was spending a lot of energy

235
00:08:42,760 --> 00:08:45,010
on questions with no answer.

236
00:08:45,060 --> 00:08:47,300
Mathieu: So you shifted your attention

237
00:08:47,300 --> 00:08:49,450
toward possible actions.

238
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:52,160
Léa: Exactly. The next day,

239
00:08:52,160 --> 00:08:53,400
I asked a question

240
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:56,000
I'd been avoiding for several days.

241
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:58,410
The answer cleared up the problem

242
00:08:58,410 --> 00:09:00,200
in a few minutes.

243
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:03,200
Mathieu: Ah! Sometimes we spend three evenings

244
00:09:03,200 --> 00:09:05,120
imagining a conversation

245
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:08,200
that only lasts five minutes in reality.

246
00:09:08,200 --> 00:09:08,750
Léa: Yes.

247
00:09:08,750 --> 00:09:12,910
And sometimes the answer is hard, of course.

248
00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:13,870
But at least,

249
00:09:13,870 --> 00:09:17,200
we have information we can act on.

250
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:19,200
Mathieu: You know, when I'm worried,

251
00:09:19,200 --> 00:09:22,900
I often confuse thinking and acting.

252
00:09:22,900 --> 00:09:25,130
I tell myself: "I still need to think about it",

253
00:09:25,130 --> 00:09:28,100
when I'm just replaying the same scenarios.

254
00:09:28,100 --> 00:09:30,900
Léa: In that case, you can ask yourself:

255
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:32,480
"What can I do

256
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:34,350
concretely today?"

257
00:09:34,400 --> 00:09:36,550
If there's no possible action,

258
00:09:36,550 --> 00:09:39,010
you can try to take a break.

259
00:09:39,060 --> 00:09:40,290
Mathieu: "Take a break",

260
00:09:40,290 --> 00:09:43,100
not "forget the problem exists".

261
00:09:43,100 --> 00:09:46,680
Léa: Exactly. We can be responsible

262
00:09:46,680 --> 00:09:49,700
without spending all night worrying.

263
00:09:49,700 --> 00:09:52,410
Mathieu: I like this idea of a positive attitude

264
00:09:52,410 --> 00:09:54,500
that keeps its feet on the ground.

265
00:09:54,500 --> 00:09:56,340
Léa: Feet on the ground, yes.

266
00:09:56,390 --> 00:09:59,000
It means being realistic,

267
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:00,660
not getting carried away

268
00:10:00,660 --> 00:10:03,600
by ideas too far from reality.

269
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:06,710
Mathieu: For example: "I hope to get this job,

270
00:10:06,710 --> 00:10:08,810
but I keep my feet on the ground

271
00:10:08,860 --> 00:10:12,000
and I'm preparing other options too."

272
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:12,950
Léa: Very good!

273
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,600
And that brings us to another topic:

274
00:10:15,600 --> 00:10:17,600
our goals.

275
00:10:17,600 --> 00:10:20,450
Do you ever compare yourself to others?

276
00:10:20,500 --> 00:10:21,840
Mathieu: Never! Well...

277
00:10:21,840 --> 00:10:23,460
only every time

278
00:10:23,460 --> 00:10:26,500
I open certain social networks.

279
00:10:26,500 --> 00:10:28,450
Léa: So, quite often.

280
00:10:28,500 --> 00:10:28,900
Mathieu: Yes.

281
00:10:28,900 --> 00:10:30,580
I see people my age

282
00:10:30,580 --> 00:10:32,510
who change careers,

283
00:10:32,560 --> 00:10:39,300
run marathons or speak five languages.

284
00:10:39,300 --> 00:10:39,840
And suddenly, I feel

285
00:10:39,840 --> 00:10:40,500
like I'm behind in my own life.

286
00:10:40,500 --> 00:10:42,440
Léa: Yet you don't always know

287
00:10:42,440 --> 00:10:43,430
their situation

288
00:10:43,430 --> 00:10:45,500
or the path they've traveled.

289
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:47,000
Mathieu: That's true.

290
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,520
I see a photo of a success,

291
00:10:49,520 --> 00:10:50,620
not the years of work

292
00:10:50,620 --> 00:10:52,500
or the hard days.

293
00:10:52,500 --> 00:10:54,980
Léa: And that comparison can keep us

294
00:10:54,980 --> 00:10:57,500
from recognizing our own progress.

295
00:10:57,500 --> 00:10:57,560
Mathieu: A few years ago,

296
00:10:57,560 --> 00:10:59,800
I wanted to learn to draw.

297
00:10:59,800 --> 00:11:02,360
I watched incredible artists

298
00:11:02,360 --> 00:11:03,500
on the Internet.

299
00:11:03,500 --> 00:11:06,050
After ten minutes with my pencil,

300
00:11:06,050 --> 00:11:08,500
I felt like quitting everything.

301
00:11:08,500 --> 00:11:10,420
Léa: You compared your first drawing

302
00:11:10,420 --> 00:11:12,450
to their professional work?

303
00:11:12,500 --> 00:11:15,500
Mathieu: Exactly. A very bad comparison.

304
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:17,230
My first dog looked

305
00:11:17,230 --> 00:11:19,500
like a potato with four legs.

306
00:11:19,500 --> 00:11:22,510
Léa: I'm sure it had charm.

307
00:11:22,560 --> 00:11:23,750
Mathieu: You're too kind!

308
00:11:23,800 --> 00:11:26,230
But one day, I found a drawing

309
00:11:26,230 --> 00:11:29,500
I'd made several weeks before.

310
00:11:29,500 --> 00:11:30,530
And I noticed

311
00:11:30,530 --> 00:11:33,500
my lines had become more precise.

312
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:35,690
Léa: You compared yourself to your old level,

313
00:11:35,690 --> 00:11:38,500
instead of comparing yourself to someone else.

314
00:11:38,500 --> 00:11:39,020
Mathieu: Yes.

315
00:11:39,020 --> 00:11:42,500
And right away, I wanted to keep going.

316
00:11:42,500 --> 00:11:45,500
Léa: That's another form of positive thinking:

317
00:11:45,500 --> 00:11:47,620
recognizing small progress

318
00:11:47,620 --> 00:11:48,620
without pretending

319
00:11:48,620 --> 00:11:50,510
you've already reached your goal.

320
00:11:50,560 --> 00:11:51,620
Mathieu: So I can say:

321
00:11:51,620 --> 00:11:54,840
"I don't draw the way I'd like to yet,

322
00:11:54,840 --> 00:11:56,500
but I'm improving."

323
00:11:56,500 --> 00:12:00,550
Léa: Exactly. The word "yet" is very useful.

324
00:12:00,550 --> 00:12:03,500
It leaves room for learning.

325
00:12:03,500 --> 00:12:05,030
Mathieu: "I don't understand yet

326
00:12:05,030 --> 00:12:07,520
every conversation in French."

327
00:12:07,520 --> 00:12:10,090
That's much more encouraging

328
00:12:10,090 --> 00:12:12,510
than "I'm hopeless at French."

329
00:12:12,560 --> 00:12:13,310
Léa: Absolutely.

330
00:12:13,360 --> 00:12:15,370
And our listeners can use it

331
00:12:15,370 --> 00:12:16,920
in their learning.

332
00:12:16,920 --> 00:12:19,620
They don't need to understand every sentence

333
00:12:19,620 --> 00:12:21,500
to notice progress.

334
00:12:21,500 --> 00:12:23,430
Mathieu: Maybe three months ago,

335
00:12:23,430 --> 00:12:26,610
they needed subtitles all the time,

336
00:12:26,610 --> 00:12:29,390
and today they understand a story

337
00:12:29,390 --> 00:12:30,500
without translating everything.

338
00:12:30,500 --> 00:12:33,370
Léa: Or they dare to ask a question in French,

339
00:12:33,370 --> 00:12:35,500
even with a few hesitations.

340
00:12:35,500 --> 00:12:38,050
Mathieu: You know, sometimes, a small victory

341
00:12:38,050 --> 00:12:40,510
can really change a day.

342
00:12:40,560 --> 00:12:40,950
Léa: Yes.

343
00:12:40,950 --> 00:12:43,470
And we can try to notice it

344
00:12:43,470 --> 00:12:45,630
instead of only thinking

345
00:12:45,630 --> 00:12:47,500
about what's left to do.

346
00:12:47,500 --> 00:12:49,610
Mathieu: But how do you stay positive

347
00:12:49,610 --> 00:12:53,450
when you feel completely stuck in life?

348
00:12:53,500 --> 00:12:55,500
Léa: That's a real question.

349
00:12:55,500 --> 00:12:57,550
You can start by avoiding

350
00:12:57,550 --> 00:13:00,500
wanting to change everything at once.

351
00:13:00,500 --> 00:13:03,220
A small direction can be more useful

352
00:13:03,220 --> 00:13:05,510
than a gigantic plan.

353
00:13:05,560 --> 00:13:08,460
Mathieu: I know all about gigantic plans.

354
00:13:08,460 --> 00:13:10,620
On Sunday night, I can

355
00:13:10,620 --> 00:13:13,500
organize a whole new life.

356
00:13:13,500 --> 00:13:17,500
On Monday morning, I negotiate with my alarm.

357
00:13:17,500 --> 00:13:20,030
Léa: Then you should make a plan

358
00:13:20,030 --> 00:13:22,250
that suits Monday's Mathieu,

359
00:13:22,250 --> 00:13:24,500
not just Sunday's!

360
00:13:24,500 --> 00:13:26,450
Mathieu: Excellent point.

361
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:29,870
For example, if I want to be more active,

362
00:13:29,870 --> 00:13:31,180
I can start

363
00:13:31,180 --> 00:13:34,510
with two short walks a week.

364
00:13:34,560 --> 00:13:37,210
Léa: Yes. And if you want to improve your French,

365
00:13:37,210 --> 00:13:39,250
you can choose a regular time

366
00:13:39,250 --> 00:13:41,500
to listen to a conversation.

367
00:13:41,500 --> 00:13:43,500
Mathieu: Not necessarily two hours a day.

368
00:13:43,500 --> 00:13:44,600
Léa: Exactly.

369
00:13:44,600 --> 00:13:46,880
Small, realistic goals

370
00:13:46,880 --> 00:13:49,010
can give you a direction

371
00:13:49,010 --> 00:13:51,500
without adding too much pressure.

372
00:13:51,500 --> 00:13:53,700
Mathieu: A friend told me something

373
00:13:53,700 --> 00:13:55,780
that often comes back to me.

374
00:13:55,780 --> 00:13:57,810
She wanted to change careers,

375
00:13:57,810 --> 00:14:00,510
but she told herself it was too late.

376
00:14:00,560 --> 00:14:04,700
Léa: How old was she?

377
00:14:04,750 --> 00:14:05,090
Mathieu: Thirty-eight.

378
00:14:05,090 --> 00:14:06,070
She worked in sales

379
00:14:06,070 --> 00:14:07,300
and had long been interested

380
00:14:07,300 --> 00:14:08,500
in interior design.

381
00:14:08,500 --> 00:14:10,450
Léa: And what was holding her back?

382
00:14:10,500 --> 00:14:12,860
Mathieu: She thought she had to quit

383
00:14:12,860 --> 00:14:14,440
her job right away,

384
00:14:14,440 --> 00:14:16,670
go back to studying for several years

385
00:14:16,670 --> 00:14:18,500
and succeed on the first try.

386
00:14:18,500 --> 00:14:21,500
That seemed impossible to her.

387
00:14:21,500 --> 00:14:23,340
Léa: She was seeing the whole road

388
00:14:23,340 --> 00:14:25,510
ahead all at once.

389
00:14:25,560 --> 00:14:26,430
Mathieu: Exactly.

390
00:14:26,430 --> 00:14:28,700
Then she met someone

391
00:14:28,700 --> 00:14:30,620
who worked in that field

392
00:14:30,620 --> 00:14:32,620
and asked them some questions.

393
00:14:32,620 --> 00:14:35,500
Next, she took an evening class.

394
00:14:35,500 --> 00:14:36,790
Léa: A first step

395
00:14:36,790 --> 00:14:39,500
that didn't turn her whole life upside down.

396
00:14:39,500 --> 00:14:41,660
Mathieu: Yes. After a few months,

397
00:14:41,660 --> 00:14:43,900
she started helping friends

398
00:14:43,900 --> 00:14:46,620
to decorate their apartments.

399
00:14:46,620 --> 00:14:49,170
Today, she's seriously exploring

400
00:14:49,170 --> 00:14:50,500
a career change,

401
00:14:50,500 --> 00:14:52,510
but she's moving forward carefully.

402
00:14:52,560 --> 00:14:54,820
Léa: And she doesn't know exactly yet

403
00:14:54,820 --> 00:14:56,500
where this project will take her.

404
00:14:56,500 --> 00:14:59,370
Mathieu: No. Still, she feels less stuck,

405
00:14:59,370 --> 00:15:02,500
because she's doing something concrete.

406
00:15:02,500 --> 00:15:04,510
Léa: I really like that example.

407
00:15:04,510 --> 00:15:07,610
Optimism isn't always about

408
00:15:07,610 --> 00:15:09,620
being sure you'll succeed.

409
00:15:09,620 --> 00:15:10,820
Sometimes, it's agreeing

410
00:15:10,820 --> 00:15:12,500
to look for a possibility.

411
00:15:12,500 --> 00:15:14,390
Mathieu: "I don't know if it'll work,

412
00:15:14,390 --> 00:15:16,550
but I can look into it."

413
00:15:16,550 --> 00:15:19,510
That's a positive and realistic sentence.

414
00:15:19,560 --> 00:15:22,200
Léa: Exactly. And it helps us

415
00:15:22,200 --> 00:15:24,620
avoid two extremes:

416
00:15:24,620 --> 00:15:26,670
believing everything will go perfectly,

417
00:15:26,670 --> 00:15:29,500
or thinking nothing can get better.

418
00:15:29,500 --> 00:15:32,700
Mathieu: In between, there's curiosity,

419
00:15:32,700 --> 00:15:35,500
trial and error, and adjustments.

420
00:15:35,500 --> 00:15:35,990
Léa: Yes.

421
00:15:35,990 --> 00:15:39,140
But we need to talk about another situation,

422
00:15:39,140 --> 00:15:40,620
a more delicate one.

423
00:15:40,620 --> 00:15:41,290
What do we do

424
00:15:41,290 --> 00:15:44,450
when we go through a real disappointment?

425
00:15:44,500 --> 00:15:48,510
Mathieu: Then, pretty phrases aren't always enough.

426
00:15:48,560 --> 00:15:49,500
Léa: That's true.

427
00:15:49,500 --> 00:15:51,100
And I really wouldn't want

428
00:15:51,100 --> 00:15:53,420
to give the impression we must smile

429
00:15:53,420 --> 00:15:55,500
after every piece of bad news.

430
00:15:55,500 --> 00:15:56,500
Mathieu: Me neither.

431
00:15:56,500 --> 00:15:58,020
I went through a time

432
00:15:58,020 --> 00:15:59,780
when a personal project

433
00:15:59,780 --> 00:16:02,210
I'd worked very hard on

434
00:16:02,210 --> 00:16:03,500
didn't come to anything.

435
00:16:03,500 --> 00:16:05,610
Léa: Had you put a lot of time

436
00:16:05,610 --> 00:16:06,450
into that project?

437
00:16:06,500 --> 00:16:08,510
Mathieu: Almost a year.

438
00:16:08,510 --> 00:16:11,090
I was hoping to organize a small exhibition

439
00:16:11,090 --> 00:16:12,510
of photos with friends.

440
00:16:12,560 --> 00:16:14,510
We'd found a venue,

441
00:16:14,510 --> 00:16:16,200
prepared a selection,

442
00:16:16,200 --> 00:16:19,500
but the event was cancelled in the end.

443
00:16:19,500 --> 00:16:21,500
Léa: You must have been very disappointed.

444
00:16:21,500 --> 00:16:24,500
Mathieu: Yes. And even angry.

445
00:16:24,500 --> 00:16:26,320
For several weeks,

446
00:16:26,320 --> 00:16:27,670
I no longer felt like

447
00:16:27,670 --> 00:16:29,500
taking out my camera.

448
00:16:29,500 --> 00:16:31,080
Léa: That's understandable.

449
00:16:31,080 --> 00:16:32,860
You'd lost something

450
00:16:32,860 --> 00:16:34,510
that mattered to you.

451
00:16:34,560 --> 00:16:35,560
Mathieu: Exactly.

452
00:16:35,560 --> 00:16:38,890
A friend called and suggested a walk.

453
00:16:38,890 --> 00:16:41,330
I told him: "I'm not in the mood

454
00:16:41,330 --> 00:16:43,450
to take nice photos."

455
00:16:43,500 --> 00:16:45,450
Léa: And what did he say?

456
00:16:45,500 --> 00:16:47,590
Mathieu: "Then don't take any photos.

457
00:16:47,590 --> 00:16:49,550
Just come for a walk."

458
00:16:49,550 --> 00:16:50,500
That was all.

459
00:16:50,500 --> 00:16:52,500
Léa: That's a very gentle answer.

460
00:16:52,500 --> 00:16:53,920
He didn't ask you

461
00:16:53,920 --> 00:16:55,960
to turn your disappointment

462
00:16:55,960 --> 00:16:58,510
into a big lesson right away.

463
00:16:58,560 --> 00:16:59,090
Mathieu: Yes.

464
00:16:59,090 --> 00:17:02,940
We walked, talked about this and that.

465
00:17:02,940 --> 00:17:05,550
After a while, I caught myself

466
00:17:05,550 --> 00:17:07,500
noticing the light on the trees.

467
00:17:07,500 --> 00:17:09,430
A few days later,

468
00:17:09,430 --> 00:17:11,500
I picked up my camera again.

469
00:17:11,500 --> 00:17:12,370
Léa: So your friend

470
00:17:12,370 --> 00:17:14,880
didn't force you to be positive.

471
00:17:14,880 --> 00:17:17,500
He just kept you company.

472
00:17:17,500 --> 00:17:18,590
Mathieu: Exactly.

473
00:17:18,590 --> 00:17:20,610
And it helped me understand

474
00:17:20,610 --> 00:17:22,950
that a disappointment can be real

475
00:17:22,950 --> 00:17:25,510
without summing up our whole future.

476
00:17:25,560 --> 00:17:27,600
Léa: I find that essential.

477
00:17:27,600 --> 00:17:28,700
Being positive

478
00:17:28,700 --> 00:17:31,640
doesn't mean denying sadness.

479
00:17:31,640 --> 00:17:33,500
You can be sad today

480
00:17:33,500 --> 00:17:35,280
and still keep the possibility

481
00:17:35,280 --> 00:17:37,500
of living something else tomorrow.

482
00:17:37,500 --> 00:17:39,730
Mathieu: And we can also ask for help

483
00:17:39,730 --> 00:17:42,620
if a situation becomes too heavy.

484
00:17:42,620 --> 00:17:45,510
We don't have to carry everything alone.

485
00:17:45,560 --> 00:17:46,480
Léa: Absolutely.

486
00:17:46,480 --> 00:17:48,030
The support of a loved one,

487
00:17:48,030 --> 00:17:50,620
and sometimes that of a professional,

488
00:17:50,620 --> 00:17:53,390
can be precious when suffering lasts

489
00:17:53,390 --> 00:17:55,500
or becomes hard to manage.

490
00:17:55,500 --> 00:17:58,070
Mathieu: You know, I like the expression

491
00:17:58,120 --> 00:18:00,450
"gradually getting your momentum back".

492
00:18:00,500 --> 00:18:03,500
Léa: Yes. "Retrouver son élan"

493
00:18:03,500 --> 00:18:05,880
means having the energy again

494
00:18:05,880 --> 00:18:07,230
or the desire to move forward

495
00:18:07,230 --> 00:18:09,510
after a difficult period.

496
00:18:09,560 --> 00:18:12,210
Mathieu: For example: "After this disappointment,

497
00:18:12,210 --> 00:18:13,780
it took him time

498
00:18:13,780 --> 00:18:15,500
to get his momentum back."

499
00:18:15,500 --> 00:18:16,320
Léa: Very good.

500
00:18:16,320 --> 00:18:18,500
And that time is different for everyone.

501
00:18:18,500 --> 00:18:20,620
We don't have to set ourselves

502
00:18:20,620 --> 00:18:22,500
a date to feel better.

503
00:18:22,500 --> 00:18:25,030
Mathieu: You know, we've talked about our thoughts,

504
00:18:25,030 --> 00:18:28,670
our goals and difficult times.

505
00:18:28,670 --> 00:18:30,500
But I think something's missing.

506
00:18:30,500 --> 00:18:32,510
Léa: What's that?

507
00:18:32,560 --> 00:18:33,270
Mathieu: Other people!

508
00:18:33,320 --> 00:18:34,730
Because a conversation

509
00:18:34,730 --> 00:18:36,740
with a kind person

510
00:18:36,740 --> 00:18:38,410
can sometimes completely change

511
00:18:38,410 --> 00:18:39,500
our day.

512
00:18:39,500 --> 00:18:40,790
Léa: I agree.

513
00:18:40,790 --> 00:18:42,250
Human relationships

514
00:18:42,250 --> 00:18:45,500
play a big part in our well-being.

515
00:18:45,500 --> 00:18:46,800
Mathieu: Last week,

516
00:18:46,800 --> 00:18:48,500
I was a bit discouraged.

517
00:18:48,500 --> 00:18:49,780
I called my sister

518
00:18:49,780 --> 00:18:51,670
to tell her about my problems,

519
00:18:51,670 --> 00:18:52,910
and she listened to me

520
00:18:52,910 --> 00:18:55,500
without trying to fix everything.

521
00:18:55,500 --> 00:18:57,220
Léa: Sometimes, being listened to

522
00:18:57,220 --> 00:18:59,510
already does a world of good.

523
00:18:59,560 --> 00:19:01,490
Mathieu: Yes. Then she told me

524
00:19:01,490 --> 00:19:03,140
that her son had wanted

525
00:19:03,140 --> 00:19:05,670
to make a cake all by himself.

526
00:19:05,670 --> 00:19:08,500
He'd mixed up the salt and the sugar.

527
00:19:08,500 --> 00:19:09,410
Léa: Oh no!

528
00:19:09,460 --> 00:19:13,500
The result must have been... surprising.

529
00:19:13,500 --> 00:19:14,790
Mathieu: Very surprising.

530
00:19:14,790 --> 00:19:17,500
Nobody asked for a second slice.

531
00:19:17,500 --> 00:19:19,510
Léa: Poor little thing!

532
00:19:19,560 --> 00:19:21,380
Mathieu: But we laughed together,

533
00:19:21,380 --> 00:19:23,780
and I felt a bit lighter.

534
00:19:23,780 --> 00:19:26,090
My problems were still there,

535
00:19:26,090 --> 00:19:29,500
but they no longer filled the whole conversation.

536
00:19:29,500 --> 00:19:31,500
Léa: That's a lovely example.

537
00:19:31,500 --> 00:19:33,100
We can acknowledge

538
00:19:33,100 --> 00:19:34,420
what worries us

539
00:19:34,420 --> 00:19:37,500
while leaving room for moments of joy.

540
00:19:37,500 --> 00:19:39,680
Mathieu: And sometimes, we can also bring

541
00:19:39,680 --> 00:19:41,500
a little of that joy to others.

542
00:19:41,500 --> 00:19:43,510
Léa: How?

543
00:19:43,560 --> 00:19:46,740
Mathieu: A kind message, a sincere compliment,

544
00:19:46,740 --> 00:19:49,440
or simply asking someone

545
00:19:49,440 --> 00:19:50,620
how they're doing

546
00:19:50,620 --> 00:19:53,500
and really listening to the answer.

547
00:19:53,500 --> 00:19:55,220
Léa: Yes. But that doesn't mean

548
00:19:55,220 --> 00:19:56,790
we're responsible

549
00:19:56,790 --> 00:19:58,500
for everyone's happiness.

550
00:19:58,500 --> 00:19:59,370
Mathieu: Of course.

551
00:19:59,370 --> 00:20:01,710
We can be thoughtful

552
00:20:01,710 --> 00:20:05,510
while respecting our energy and our limits.

553
00:20:05,560 --> 00:20:06,510
Léa: Exactly.

554
00:20:06,510 --> 00:20:09,740
And sometimes, a small gesture also reminds us

555
00:20:09,740 --> 00:20:12,260
that there are still nice things

556
00:20:12,260 --> 00:20:14,500
in a complicated day.

557
00:20:14,500 --> 00:20:16,220
Mathieu: You know, I've started

558
00:20:16,220 --> 00:20:18,520
a little habit in the evening.

559
00:20:18,520 --> 00:20:19,620
I write down one thing

560
00:20:19,620 --> 00:20:22,500
that went well during the day.

561
00:20:22,500 --> 00:20:23,450
Léa: Just one?

562
00:20:23,500 --> 00:20:24,500
Mathieu: Yes.

563
00:20:24,500 --> 00:20:27,410
It can be a good conversation,

564
00:20:27,410 --> 00:20:29,030
a meal I enjoyed

565
00:20:29,030 --> 00:20:31,500
or a task finally finished.

566
00:20:31,500 --> 00:20:33,510
Léa: And you do that every day?

567
00:20:33,560 --> 00:20:34,450
Mathieu: Not always.

568
00:20:34,450 --> 00:20:36,500
I don't make it an obligation.

569
00:20:36,500 --> 00:20:38,620
But when I think of it,

570
00:20:38,620 --> 00:20:41,390
it helps me look at my day

571
00:20:41,390 --> 00:20:43,500
with a bit more nuance.

572
00:20:43,500 --> 00:20:45,510
Léa: I like that approach.

573
00:20:45,510 --> 00:20:47,210
Gratitude shouldn't become

574
00:20:47,210 --> 00:20:49,500
a way to blame yourself for being sad.

575
00:20:49,500 --> 00:20:51,510
Mathieu: Exactly.

576
00:20:51,510 --> 00:20:54,370
Some days, my only sentence is:

577
00:20:54,420 --> 00:20:56,260
"I finally got to rest."

578
00:20:56,310 --> 00:20:58,510
And that's already enough.

579
00:20:58,560 --> 00:21:00,950
Léa: You know, that reminds me of someone

580
00:21:00,950 --> 00:21:02,080
who once told me:

581
00:21:02,100 --> 00:21:03,420
"A bad day

582
00:21:03,420 --> 00:21:05,450
can contain a good moment."

583
00:21:05,500 --> 00:21:07,500
Mathieu: I really like that.

584
00:21:07,500 --> 00:21:09,780
And the opposite is true too:

585
00:21:09,780 --> 00:21:10,840
a good day

586
00:21:10,840 --> 00:21:13,500
can contain a difficult moment.

587
00:21:13,500 --> 00:21:13,940
Léa: Yes.

588
00:21:13,940 --> 00:21:17,080
We don't need to label every day

589
00:21:17,080 --> 00:21:18,770
as a complete success

590
00:21:18,770 --> 00:21:20,510
or a total failure.

591
00:21:20,560 --> 00:21:21,790
Mathieu: That's reassuring.

592
00:21:21,790 --> 00:21:23,940
Because mixed days,

593
00:21:23,940 --> 00:21:26,470
with good and not-so-good things,

594
00:21:26,470 --> 00:21:29,500
are probably the most common.

595
00:21:29,500 --> 00:21:31,510
Léa: Absolutely.

596
00:21:31,510 --> 00:21:33,860
And learning to see those different shades

597
00:21:33,860 --> 00:21:35,720
is already a way to develop

598
00:21:35,720 --> 00:21:37,500
a more balanced attitude.

599
00:21:37,500 --> 00:21:39,800
Mathieu: So, could we say

600
00:21:39,800 --> 00:21:41,780
that positive thinking means

601
00:21:41,780 --> 00:21:43,260
looking for light

602
00:21:43,260 --> 00:21:46,510
without pretending the shadow doesn't exist?

603
00:21:46,560 --> 00:21:48,170
Léa: That's a beautiful image.

604
00:21:48,170 --> 00:21:51,500
And I think our listeners will remember it.

605
00:21:51,500 --> 00:21:52,060
Mathieu: Thank you!

606
00:21:52,060 --> 00:21:53,600
Maybe I'll write it down

607
00:21:53,600 --> 00:21:55,500
in my notebook of quotes.

608
00:21:55,500 --> 00:21:56,840
Léa: The one that already holds

609
00:21:56,840 --> 00:21:58,450
your cooking recipes?

610
00:21:58,500 --> 00:21:59,020
Mathieu: Yes.

611
00:21:59,020 --> 00:22:00,910
At my place, philosophy

612
00:22:00,910 --> 00:22:03,060
and potato gratin

613
00:22:03,060 --> 00:22:05,500
live in perfect harmony.

614
00:22:05,500 --> 00:22:08,500
Léa: That doesn't surprise me at all!

615
00:22:08,500 --> 00:22:10,390
Mathieu: Well, Léa, we've used

616
00:22:10,390 --> 00:22:13,620
lots of lovely expressions today.

617
00:22:13,620 --> 00:22:14,510
Shall we review them?

618
00:22:14,560 --> 00:22:18,830
Léa: With pleasure. Let's start with

619
00:22:18,880 --> 00:22:21,450
"voir le bon côté des choses".

620
00:22:21,500 --> 00:22:23,490
Mathieu: This expression means

621
00:22:23,530 --> 00:22:25,780
noticing what's still positive

622
00:22:25,780 --> 00:22:27,450
in a situation.

623
00:22:27,500 --> 00:22:30,030
For example: "I missed my train,

624
00:22:30,030 --> 00:22:33,470
but I try to look on the bright side:

625
00:22:33,470 --> 00:22:34,500
I have time to read."

626
00:22:34,500 --> 00:22:36,500
Léa: Careful, it doesn't mean

627
00:22:36,500 --> 00:22:39,090
you have to find something positive

628
00:22:39,090 --> 00:22:41,510
in every painful situation.

629
00:22:41,560 --> 00:22:43,600
It's mainly a useful expression

630
00:22:43,600 --> 00:22:45,500
for small setbacks.

631
00:22:45,500 --> 00:22:47,750
Mathieu: Second expression:

632
00:22:47,750 --> 00:22:49,500
"prendre du recul".

633
00:22:49,500 --> 00:22:50,610
Léa: It means looking at a situation

634
00:22:50,610 --> 00:22:55,450
with more distance and calm.

635
00:22:55,500 --> 00:22:58,500
For example: "After a bad day,

636
00:22:58,500 --> 00:23:00,420
I need to step back

637
00:23:00,420 --> 00:23:02,500
before making a decision."

638
00:23:02,500 --> 00:23:04,380
Mathieu: Third expression:

639
00:23:04,380 --> 00:23:06,560
"garder les pieds sur terre".

640
00:23:06,560 --> 00:23:09,310
Léa: It means staying realistic.

641
00:23:09,360 --> 00:23:10,550
For example:

642
00:23:10,550 --> 00:23:13,620
"I'm excited about this project,

643
00:23:13,620 --> 00:23:15,500
but I keep my feet on the ground."

644
00:23:15,500 --> 00:23:17,740
Mathieu: Fourth expression:

645
00:23:17,740 --> 00:23:19,500
"avoir tendance à".

646
00:23:19,500 --> 00:23:19,500
Léa: It means

647
00:23:19,500 --> 00:23:23,640
doing something quite often.

648
00:23:23,690 --> 00:23:24,250
For example:

649
00:23:24,250 --> 00:23:25,610
"When I'm tired,

650
00:23:25,610 --> 00:23:27,500
I tend to imagine the worst."

651
00:23:27,500 --> 00:23:29,580
Mathieu: Fifth expression:

652
00:23:29,580 --> 00:23:31,560
"reprendre confiance".

653
00:23:31,560 --> 00:23:33,930
Léa: It's gradually getting back

654
00:23:33,930 --> 00:23:36,570
the confidence you had lost.

655
00:23:36,620 --> 00:23:37,680
For example:

656
00:23:37,680 --> 00:23:39,500
"After several small successes,

657
00:23:39,500 --> 00:23:42,500
she began to regain confidence."

658
00:23:42,500 --> 00:23:44,940
Mathieu: And our last expression:

659
00:23:44,940 --> 00:23:46,500
"retrouver son élan".

660
00:23:46,500 --> 00:23:46,500
Léa: It means

661
00:23:46,500 --> 00:23:50,710
finding the energy to keep going again.

662
00:23:50,760 --> 00:23:51,620
For example:

663
00:23:51,620 --> 00:23:53,910
"After a difficult period,

664
00:23:53,910 --> 00:23:55,430
he got his momentum back

665
00:23:55,430 --> 00:23:57,500
thanks to a new project."

666
00:23:57,500 --> 00:23:59,340
Mathieu: There you go, six very useful expressions

667
00:23:59,340 --> 00:24:00,790
to talk about our emotions

668
00:24:00,790 --> 00:24:02,500
and our way of seeing life.

669
00:24:02,500 --> 00:24:02,500
Léa: Now, let's see how

670
00:24:02,500 --> 00:24:05,940
to replace some very general sentences

671
00:24:05,940 --> 00:24:10,500
with more natural wording.

672
00:24:10,500 --> 00:24:12,930
Mathieu: First sentence:

673
00:24:12,930 --> 00:24:14,500
"Everything's going wrong."

674
00:24:14,500 --> 00:24:14,500
Léa: You can say:

675
00:24:14,500 --> 00:24:15,490
"I'm having a complicated day."

676
00:24:15,540 --> 00:24:17,700
Or: "Several things

677
00:24:17,700 --> 00:24:21,910
aren't going as planned today."

678
00:24:21,910 --> 00:24:23,680
Mathieu: It's more precise,

679
00:24:23,680 --> 00:24:25,910
and it doesn't turn

680
00:24:25,910 --> 00:24:29,560
a bad morning into a bad life.

681
00:24:29,560 --> 00:24:31,520
Léa: Second sentence:

682
00:24:31,570 --> 00:24:32,450
"I'm hopeless."

683
00:24:32,500 --> 00:24:33,730
Mathieu: You can say:

684
00:24:33,780 --> 00:24:36,570
"I still lack practice."

685
00:24:36,620 --> 00:24:38,690
Or: "I didn't succeed this time,

686
00:24:38,690 --> 00:24:40,340
but I can learn."

687
00:24:40,340 --> 00:24:42,910
Léa: Third sentence:

688
00:24:42,910 --> 00:24:46,500
"I always think the worst."

689
00:24:46,500 --> 00:24:46,500
Mathieu: You can say:

690
00:24:46,500 --> 00:24:47,360
"I tend to anticipate problems."

691
00:24:47,410 --> 00:24:50,120
Or: "I sometimes worry

692
00:24:50,120 --> 00:24:53,720
too much about the future."

693
00:24:53,720 --> 00:24:55,990
Léa: Fourth sentence:

694
00:24:55,990 --> 00:24:58,560
"I want to be happy."

695
00:24:58,560 --> 00:24:59,410
Mathieu: You can say:

696
00:24:59,460 --> 00:25:01,900
"I'd like to appreciate more

697
00:25:01,900 --> 00:25:04,620
the little everyday moments."

698
00:25:04,670 --> 00:25:07,290
Léa: Or: "I'm looking for a better balance

699
00:25:07,290 --> 00:25:08,170
in my life."

700
00:25:08,170 --> 00:25:10,080
Mathieu: Fifth sentence:

701
00:25:10,080 --> 00:25:12,500
"I can't change anything."

702
00:25:12,500 --> 00:25:12,500
Léa: You can say:

703
00:25:12,500 --> 00:25:13,490
"I can't control everything,

704
00:25:13,490 --> 00:25:17,840
but I can act on some things."

705
00:25:17,840 --> 00:25:20,610
Mathieu: And the last sentence:

706
00:25:20,610 --> 00:25:23,560
"I'm afraid to try."

707
00:25:23,560 --> 00:25:24,320
Léa: You can say:

708
00:25:24,340 --> 00:25:26,710
"I'm not very confident yet,

709
00:25:26,710 --> 00:25:29,070
but I'd like to take a first step."

710
00:25:29,070 --> 00:25:32,200
Mathieu: You know, I really like these alternatives.

711
00:25:32,200 --> 00:25:34,080
They're more honest

712
00:25:34,080 --> 00:25:36,500
than pretending everything's fine.

713
00:25:36,500 --> 00:25:37,500
Léa: Exactly.

714
00:25:37,500 --> 00:25:38,910
And they can also help

715
00:25:38,910 --> 00:25:40,710
when you're learning French.

716
00:25:40,710 --> 00:25:41,810
Instead of saying

717
00:25:41,860 --> 00:25:44,450
"I'll never speak well",

718
00:25:44,500 --> 00:25:47,280
you can say "I'm going at my own pace,

719
00:25:47,280 --> 00:25:50,040
and I already understand more than before."

720
00:25:50,040 --> 00:25:51,960
Mathieu: Little sentences matter.

721
00:25:51,960 --> 00:25:54,500
Especially the ones we repeat to ourselves.

722
00:25:54,500 --> 00:25:54,500
Léa: Yes. But let's remember:

723
00:25:54,500 --> 00:25:56,490
nobody controls their thoughts

724
00:25:56,490 --> 00:25:58,190
all the time.

725
00:25:58,190 --> 00:26:00,680
It's not an exercise

726
00:26:00,680 --> 00:26:04,500
where you have to get a perfect grade.

727
00:26:04,500 --> 00:26:05,470
Mathieu: Thank goodness,

728
00:26:05,470 --> 00:26:08,310
because my brain would already have asked

729
00:26:08,310 --> 00:26:10,500
for a second chance at the exam!

730
00:26:10,500 --> 00:26:10,500
Léa: Mathieu, if you had to remember

731
00:26:10,500 --> 00:26:14,080
just one thing from our conversation,

732
00:26:14,080 --> 00:26:15,940
what would it be?

733
00:26:15,940 --> 00:26:19,440
Mathieu: That I can have an imperfect day

734
00:26:19,440 --> 00:26:23,560
without deciding it's completely bad.

735
00:26:23,560 --> 00:26:24,790
And that sometimes,

736
00:26:24,790 --> 00:26:27,520
one small action changes more

737
00:26:27,520 --> 00:26:29,500
than an hour of worrying.

738
00:26:29,500 --> 00:26:30,880
Léa: Me, I'd remember

739
00:26:30,880 --> 00:26:33,200
that we can look for hope

740
00:26:33,200 --> 00:26:35,610
while acknowledging pain,

741
00:26:35,610 --> 00:26:37,500
tiredness or disappointment.

742
00:26:37,500 --> 00:26:37,500
Mathieu: So being positive

743
00:26:37,500 --> 00:26:42,500
isn't smiling no matter what.

744
00:26:42,500 --> 00:26:42,790
Léa: No.

745
00:26:42,790 --> 00:26:44,510
Sometimes it's telling yourself:

746
00:26:44,510 --> 00:26:46,820
"It's hard right now,

747
00:26:46,820 --> 00:26:48,160
but I'll see

748
00:26:48,160 --> 00:26:50,560
what can help me move forward."

749
00:26:50,560 --> 00:26:53,700
Mathieu: And for my next disastrous morning,

750
00:26:53,700 --> 00:26:55,790
I'll try not to let

751
00:26:55,790 --> 00:26:58,770
a cup of coffee decide my mood

752
00:26:58,770 --> 00:27:00,500
for twelve hours.

753
00:27:00,500 --> 00:27:02,510
Léa: That's an excellent goal!

754
00:27:02,510 --> 00:27:04,870
But maybe you should also

755
00:27:04,870 --> 00:27:07,500
buy a spare shirt.

756
00:27:07,500 --> 00:27:09,450
Mathieu: Such wisdom, Léa.

757
00:27:09,500 --> 00:27:12,250
Positive thinking finally meets

758
00:27:12,250 --> 00:27:14,500
practical organization.

759
00:27:14,500 --> 00:27:17,510
Léa: The two can work very well together!

760
00:27:17,560 --> 00:27:20,450
Mathieu: So, dear friends, it's your turn.

761
00:27:20,500 --> 00:27:22,680
What little positive habit

762
00:27:22,680 --> 00:27:25,450
would you like to try this week?

763
00:27:25,500 --> 00:27:28,560
Léa: In the comments, you can write:

764
00:27:28,610 --> 00:27:30,620
"This week, I'd like

765
00:27:30,620 --> 00:27:32,500
to get into the habit of..."

766
00:27:32,500 --> 00:27:34,060
Mathieu: Or: "Today,

767
00:27:34,060 --> 00:27:37,620
one thing that did me good was..."

768
00:27:37,620 --> 00:27:40,050
One or two sentences are enough.

769
00:27:40,050 --> 00:27:42,510
Don't be afraid of mistakes.

770
00:27:42,560 --> 00:27:45,010
Léa: And if you enjoyed this conversation,

771
00:27:45,010 --> 00:27:46,300
remember to like it,

772
00:27:46,300 --> 00:27:48,660
to subscribe to The Cozy French

773
00:27:48,660 --> 00:27:49,620
and to share it

774
00:27:49,620 --> 00:27:52,500
with someone who's learning French.

775
00:27:52,500 --> 00:27:54,500
Mathieu: Thank you for spending this time with us.

776
00:27:54,500 --> 00:27:57,890
Whether you're going through a good time

777
00:27:57,890 --> 00:27:59,500
or a harder one,

778
00:27:59,500 --> 00:28:02,210
we hope you've discovered

779
00:28:02,210 --> 00:28:04,250
a few useful expressions

780
00:28:04,250 --> 00:28:06,510
and an idea to keep for yourself.

781
00:28:06,560 --> 00:28:07,940
Léa: Take care of yourselves,

782
00:28:07,940 --> 00:28:10,840
go at your own pace and remember:

783
00:28:10,840 --> 00:28:12,470
you don't need

784
00:28:12,470 --> 00:28:14,500
to be happy every moment

785
00:28:14,500 --> 00:28:16,090
to enjoy the good things

786
00:28:16,090 --> 00:28:17,500
when they come along.

787
00:28:17,500 --> 00:28:18,500
Mathieu: This was Mathieu...

788
00:28:18,500 --> 00:28:21,500
Léa: And Léa, from The Cozy French!

789
00:28:21,500 --> 00:28:22,930
Mathieu: See you very soon

790
00:28:22,930 --> 00:28:25,500
for a new conversation in French.

791
00:28:25,500 --> 00:28:27,460
Léa: See you very soon, dear friends!

792
00:28:27,510 --> 00:28:29,220
Mathieu: See you soon!
